// Unit 2 "Stocks", Chapter 2: Reading the Numbers
export default {
  id: 'stocks-ch2',
  title: 'Reading the Numbers',
  blurb: 'The three financial statements, earnings season, valuation ratios and quality checks, all learned on Ms. Ortiz\'s bakery.',
  lessons: [
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
    {
      id: 'stocks-2-income-statement',
      title: 'The Income Statement: From Croissants to Profit',
      summary: 'Follow every dollar Ortiz Bakeries brings in, from revenue down through costs, interest and taxes to the bottom line.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'receipt',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Three locations later', html: `<p>Ortiz Bakeries Inc. has grown. It now runs <strong>three locations</strong> and sells bread wholesale to cafés. Along the way it did a 100-for-1 split, so it now has <strong>100,000 shares</strong> outstanding, and Maya's 10 shares became 1,000. Still 1%.</p><p>Maya wants to know: is this business actually making money? For that, every company publishes three core <span class="term" data-def="Standardized reports showing a company's results and financial position: the income statement, balance sheet and cash flow statement.">financial statements</span>. The first one is the <span class="term" data-def="A report of revenue, expenses and profit over a period, like a quarter or a year. Also called the profit and loss statement, or P&L.">income statement</span>.</p><p>The income statement covers a <strong>period of time</strong>, like a quarter or a year. It starts with all the money customers paid and subtracts costs, layer by layer, until only profit is left. That is why people call revenue the "top line" and net income the "bottom line."</p><p>Ortiz Bakeries is a made-up company, but the layout is real. Open the annual report of any public company, from a coffee chain to a chipmaker, and you will find the same waterfall with the same names. Learn it once on a bakery and you can read it anywhere.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "The income statement is a waterfall. Money pours in at the top and every cost takes a bucket on the way down." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'income-statement', caption: 'Revenue at the top, then cost of goods sold, operating expenses, interest and taxes are subtracted in order. Notice the three profit levels: gross, operating and net.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Top: revenue and cost of goods sold', html: `<p>Here is the bakery's year, in dollars:</p><table><tbody><tr><td><strong>Revenue</strong></td><td>2,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cost of goods sold</td><td>-800,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gross profit</strong></td><td>1,200,000</td></tr></tbody></table><p><span class="term" data-def="All the money a company earned from selling its products or services in the period, before any costs. Also called sales or the top line.">Revenue</span> is every dollar of bread, pastries and wholesale orders sold.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="Costs directly tied to making the products sold: ingredients, packaging, and the wages of people making the goods.">Cost of goods sold</span> (COGS) is what it directly cost to make those goods: flour, butter, sugar, boxes and the wages of the bakers in the kitchen.</p><p>Subtract one from the other and you get <strong>gross profit</strong>: what is left after making the product, before running the rest of the business. The bakery keeps $1.2 million of every $2 million, a <span class="hl">60% gross margin</span>.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Lumen Bikes had revenue of $50 million and cost of goods sold of $35 million. What is its gross margin, in percent?', answer: 30, tolerance: 0.1, unit: '%', explain: 'Gross profit = $50M - $35M = $15M. Gross margin = $15M / $50M = 30%. Bikes have far more expensive parts per dollar of sales than bread does.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Middle: operating expenses', html: `<p>Gross profit still has to pay for running the business. Those are <span class="term" data-def="Costs of running the business that are not directly part of making the product: rent, office staff, marketing, research, depreciation.">operating expenses</span>:</p><table><tbody><tr><td>Rent for three shops</td><td>-300,000</td></tr><tr><td>Counter staff, manager, bookkeeper</td><td>-350,000</td></tr><tr><td>Marketing</td><td>-50,000</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation of ovens and fit-outs</td><td>-100,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Operating income</strong></td><td><strong>400,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p><span class="term" data-def="Profit from the core business after all operating costs, before interest and taxes. Often close to EBIT.">Operating income</span> is the profit from actually running the bakery. It is often the best single number for judging the core business, because it ignores how the company is financed and how it is taxed.</p><p>That <strong>depreciation</strong> line is interesting. The bakery did not write a $100,000 check for it this year. It spreads the cost of ovens bought earlier over their useful life. Remember that. It returns in the cash flow lesson.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Depreciation is a real cost spread over time. The oven wears out whether or not a check is written this year." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Which of these would most likely be counted in cost of goods sold for the bakery, rather than in operating expenses?', options: ['The bookkeeper salary', 'Butter used in croissants', 'An online ad campaign', 'Rent on the main office'], answer: 1, explain: 'Ingredients go straight into the product, so they belong in COGS. Bookkeeping, marketing and office rent are costs of running the business, so they are operating expenses.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Bottom: interest, taxes, net income', html: `<p>Two more groups get paid before the owners:</p><table><tbody><tr><td><strong>Operating income</strong></td><td>400,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on the bank loan</td><td>-30,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pretax income</strong></td><td>370,000</td></tr><tr><td>Income taxes</td><td>-90,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Net income</strong></td><td><strong>280,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The lender gets its interest. The government gets its taxes. What remains, <span class="term" data-def="Profit left after every expense, interest and tax. The bottom line, which belongs to shareholders.">net income</span>, belongs to the shareholders. The board can pay it out as dividends or reinvest it.</p><p>Now divide by shares: $280,000 / 100,000 shares = <span class="hl">$2.80 earnings per share</span>. Maya's 1,000 shares have a claim on $2,800 of this year's profit, even if not a cent of it is paid to her.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Using the bakery numbers ($280,000 net income on $2,000,000 revenue), what is the net profit margin, in percent?', answer: 14, tolerance: 0.1, unit: '%', explain: '$280,000 / $2,000,000 = 0.14, or 14%. Out of every dollar a customer pays, about 14 cents ends up as profit for the owners.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'EBIT and EBITDA', html: `<p>You will see two acronyms everywhere. <strong>EBIT</strong> is earnings before interest and taxes, close to operating income: $400,000 for the bakery. <strong>EBITDA</strong> also adds back depreciation and amortization: $400,000 + $100,000 = $500,000. EBITDA is useful for comparing companies with different equipment and debt levels, but it ignores the real cost of replacing worn-out ovens.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Watch out for "adjusted" profits', html: `<p>Official results follow <span class="term" data-def="Generally Accepted Accounting Principles: the standard accounting rules US public companies must follow.">GAAP</span>, the US accounting rulebook. Many companies also highlight their own "adjusted" or <span class="term" data-def="A company's own profit measure that excludes some costs GAAP includes, such as stock-based compensation or one-time charges.">non-GAAP</span> numbers that leave out certain costs, like restructuring charges or stock-based compensation.</p><p>Sometimes that is reasonable. A truly one-time cost, like a lawsuit settlement, can distort a single year. But some companies have "one-time" charges every single year, and stock-based compensation is a real cost to owners, as you learned in the last chapter.</p><p>Companies must show how they get from GAAP to adjusted numbers in a <strong>reconciliation</strong>. If the gap is large and growing, be skeptical about which profit number tells the truth.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "If a company's adjusted profit is always way higher than its real profit, ask what costs they keep wishing away." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'The gift card puzzle', html: `<p>In December, Ortiz Bakeries sells $20,000 of gift cards. The cash is in the bank, but is it revenue? Not yet. Under <span class="term" data-def="Recording revenue when it is earned and costs when they are incurred, not when cash moves.">accrual accounting</span>, revenue counts when the bakery actually <em>delivers</em> the bread, which happens when customers redeem the cards. Until then, the $20,000 is a liability: the bakery owes those customers pastries. This is the first hint that profit and cash are not the same thing.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Read it like an investor', html: `<p>One year of numbers is a photo. Investors want the movie. Put several years side by side and ask:</p><ul><li><strong>Is revenue growing?</strong> Last year the bakery sold $1.6 million. This year $2.0 million. That is <span class="up">+25%</span> growth, driven by the third location.</li><li><strong>Are margins holding?</strong> If revenue grows but gross margin slides from 60% to 50%, maybe butter prices jumped or the bakery is discounting to win sales.</li><li><strong>Is profit growing faster or slower than sales?</strong> When costs like rent and the manager's salary stay mostly fixed, extra sales can drop to the bottom line quickly. That effect is called <span class="term" data-def="When fixed costs stay flat as sales grow, so profits grow faster than revenue. Works in reverse when sales fall.">operating leverage</span>, and it cuts both ways when sales fall.</li></ul><p>Companies also report the same quarter a year earlier for comparison, because seasons matter. A bakery's December is not comparable to its February.</p>` },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'Put these income statement lines in order from top to bottom.', items: ['Revenue', 'Gross profit', 'Operating income', 'Pretax income', 'Net income'], explain: 'Subtract COGS to get gross profit, operating expenses to get operating income, interest to get pretax income, then taxes to get net income.' },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each bakery item to where it shows up on the income statement.', pairs: [ { left: 'Wholesale bread orders from cafés', right: 'Revenue' }, { left: 'Flour and sugar', right: 'Cost of goods sold' }, { left: 'Rent on the shops', right: 'Operating expenses' }, { left: 'Interest on the bank loan', right: 'Below operating income' } ] },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Revenue', back: 'Total sales in the period. The top line.' }, { front: 'Gross profit', back: 'Revenue minus the direct cost of making the goods.' }, { front: 'Operating income', back: 'Profit from the core business, before interest and taxes.' }, { front: 'Net income', back: 'Profit after all costs, interest and taxes. The bottom line.' }, { front: 'Depreciation', back: 'Spreading the cost of long-lasting equipment over its useful life.' }, { front: 'Non-GAAP', back: 'A company-defined profit measure that excludes certain costs. Read with care.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What does the income statement measure?', options: ['What a company owns and owes on one day', 'Revenue, expenses and profit over a period of time', 'Only the cash that moved in and out', 'The share price history'], answer: 1, explain: 'The income statement covers a period, like a quarter or year. A snapshot of what is owned and owed is the balance sheet.' },
          { q: 'Pebble Coffee has revenue of $10M, COGS of $4M and operating expenses of $3.5M. What is operating income?', options: ['$6M', '$3.5M', '$2.5M', '$4M'], answer: 2, explain: 'Gross profit = $10M - $4M = $6M. Operating income = $6M - $3.5M = $2.5M.' },
          { q: 'Why do analysts often focus on operating income?', options: ['It shows core business profit before financing and tax choices', 'It includes all one-time gains', 'It is always the largest profit number', 'It is the only number the SEC requires'], answer: 0, explain: 'Operating income strips out interest and taxes, so it compares the core business more fairly across companies with different debt and tax situations.' },
          { q: 'A company has net income of $50 million and 25 million shares outstanding. What is EPS?', options: ['$0.50', '$1.25', '$5.00', '$2.00'], answer: 3, explain: 'EPS = net income / shares = $50M / 25M = $2.00.' },
          { q: 'What is the main danger of relying on adjusted (non-GAAP) earnings?', options: ['They are illegal', 'Companies may exclude real, recurring costs to make profits look better', 'They are always lower than GAAP', 'They ignore revenue'], answer: 1, explain: 'Adjusted figures can be useful, but they can also hide recurring costs like stock-based compensation or constant restructuring.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
    {
      id: 'stocks-2-balance-sheet',
      title: 'The Balance Sheet: What It Owns, What It Owes',
      summary: 'A snapshot of everything a company owns and owes on one day. Learn the equation that always balances, plus cash, debt and book value.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'scale',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'A photo, not a movie', html: `<p>The income statement was a movie: what happened over a year. The <span class="term" data-def="A snapshot of what a company owns (assets), owes (liabilities) and what is left for owners (equity) on a specific date.">balance sheet</span> is a photo taken on one specific day, usually the last day of a quarter or year.</p><p>It answers three questions about Ortiz Bakeries on December 31:</p><ul><li>What does it <strong>own</strong>? Those are <span class="term" data-def="Things a company owns or controls that have value: cash, inventory, equipment, buildings, money owed by customers.">assets</span>.</li><li>What does it <strong>owe</strong>? Those are <span class="term" data-def="What a company owes to others: loans, unpaid bills, wages owed, deferred revenue.">liabilities</span>.</li><li>What is left for the <strong>owners</strong>? That is <span class="term" data-def="Assets minus liabilities. The owners' accounting stake in the company. Also called shareholders' equity or book value.">equity</span>.</li></ul><p>And it always, always balances:</p><p><span class="hl">Assets = Liabilities + Equity</span></p><p>Every asset was paid for either with borrowed money or with the owners' money. No exceptions.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'happy', say: "Assets equal liabilities plus equity. Every single time. If it does not balance, someone made a mistake." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'balance-sheet', caption: 'Assets on one side, liabilities and equity on the other, always the same total. Notice how each side is split into short-term and long-term pieces.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Assets: short-term and long-term', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th>$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cash</td><td>360,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable (cafés that owe for bread)</td><td>100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory (flour, butter, boxes)</td><td>40,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total current assets</strong></td><td><strong>500,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Ovens, mixers and shop fit-outs, after depreciation</td><td>1,500,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td><strong>2,000,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p><span class="term" data-def="Assets expected to turn into cash or be used up within about a year: cash, receivables, inventory.">Current assets</span> are cash or things that should become cash within about a year. <span class="term" data-def="Money customers owe the company for goods already delivered.">Accounts receivable</span> is bread already delivered to cafés that have not paid yet.</p><p>Long-term assets, like ovens, last for years. They are listed at what they cost minus the depreciation taken so far, not at what they could be sold for today.</p><p>Notice the order. US balance sheets usually list assets from the most liquid, cash, down to the least liquid, like buildings and equipment. So the closer to the top, the faster it can pay a bill. A company whose assets are mostly at the bottom of the list can be rich on paper and still short of cash.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Liabilities: short-term and long-term', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>Liabilities</th><th>$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accounts payable (owed to the flour supplier)</td><td>120,000</td></tr><tr><td>Wages owed and unredeemed gift cards</td><td>80,000</td></tr><tr><td>Loan payments due this year</td><td>50,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total current liabilities</strong></td><td><strong>250,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan, due after this year</td><td>450,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total liabilities</strong></td><td><strong>700,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p><span class="term" data-def="Obligations due within about a year: unpaid bills, wages owed, short-term debt.">Current liabilities</span> must be paid within about a year. Notice the gift cards from last lesson living here: the bakery owes pastries.</p><p>Not every liability is <strong>debt</strong>. Debt means borrowed money that charges interest, here $50,000 + $450,000 = <span class="hl">$500,000 of total debt</span>. Bills to suppliers are liabilities, but not debt.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: "Using the tables, what is Ortiz Bakeries' shareholders' equity (total assets minus total liabilities), in dollars?", answer: 1300000, tolerance: 1, unit: '$', explain: "$2,000,000 assets - $700,000 liabilities = $1,300,000 of equity. That is the owners' accounting stake." },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Equity and book value', html: `<p>The bakery's <strong>$1,300,000 of equity</strong> came from two places:</p><ul><li><strong>Paid-in capital:</strong> money owners put in by buying shares, here $300,000.</li><li><span class="term" data-def="All the profits a company has kept over its life instead of paying out as dividends.">Retained earnings</span>: profits kept in the business over the years instead of paid out, here $1,000,000.</li></ul><p>Equity is also called <span class="term" data-def="The accounting value of shareholders' equity. Assets minus liabilities.">book value</span>. Divide by shares and you get <strong>book value per share</strong>: $1,300,000 / 100,000 = <strong>$13</strong>.</p><p>Equity can even go <em>negative</em>. Some companies that borrowed heavily to buy back lots of stock, or lost money for years, have liabilities larger than their assets on paper. That is not always a disaster for a highly profitable business, but it is always worth understanding why.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: "Book value is an accounting number, not a price tag. The market can value a company way above or below it." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: "Leo's personal balance sheet", html: `<p>The same equation works for people. Leo owns a used car worth about $12,000 and has $5,000 in savings: $17,000 of assets. He owes $20,000 in student loans. His equity, which for a person we call <strong>net worth</strong>, is $17,000 - $20,000 = <span class="down">-$3,000</span>. Totally normal at 22. Each loan payment and each dollar saved moves him toward positive. Companies work the same way, just with more zeros.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Ortiz Bakeries borrows $200,000 from the bank and leaves the money in its account for now. What happens to the balance sheet?', options: ['Assets and liabilities both rise by $200,000, equity unchanged', 'Equity rises by $200,000', 'Assets rise, and nothing else changes', 'Liabilities rise, and equity falls by $200,000'], answer: 0, explain: 'Cash (an asset) goes up $200,000 and the loan (a liability) goes up $200,000. The owners are not richer or poorer, so equity is unchanged. The equation still balances.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Can it pay its bills?', html: `<p>Investors look at the balance sheet first to answer a scary question: <em>could this company run out of money?</em></p><p>Two quick tools:</p><ul><li><span class="term" data-def="Current assets minus current liabilities. A cushion for paying short-term obligations.">Working capital</span> = current assets - current liabilities. The bakery: $500,000 - $250,000 = <strong>$250,000</strong>.</li><li><strong>Current ratio</strong> = current assets / current liabilities = $500,000 / $250,000 = <strong>2.0</strong>. The bakery has $2 of near-term resources for every $1 due soon.</li></ul><p>Then look at debt. $500,000 of debt against $360,000 of cash means <span class="term" data-def="Total debt minus cash. How much debt would remain if the company used all its cash to pay it down.">net debt</span> of just $140,000. Very manageable. A company with huge debt, little cash and loans due soon is in a much shakier spot, even if its income statement looks fine.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Companies rarely die from low profits alone. They die when the cash runs out and the loans come due. Check the balance sheet." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'What the balance sheet misses', html: `<p>The bakery's secret recipes, loyal customers and great reputation are worth a lot, yet they do not show up as assets, because they were built internally rather than bought. That is why book value can badly understate brand-heavy or software businesses.</p><p>The reverse happens with <span class="term" data-def="An asset created when a company buys another for more than the value of its identifiable assets. It is written down if the deal turns out worse than expected.">goodwill</span>. When a company overpays for an acquisition, the extra shows up as goodwill. If the deal sours, goodwill gets written down, and that loss hits the income statement.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'How the statements connect', html: `<p>The balance sheet and income statement are wired together. Here is the main wire: <strong>net income flows into retained earnings</strong>.</p><p>This year the bakery earned $280,000 and paid $60,000 in dividends. So retained earnings rose by $280,000 - $60,000 = <span class="up">$220,000</span>. Profit the owners left in the business shows up as more equity, and as more assets (cash, ovens) on the other side.</p><p>Another wire: the $100,000 of depreciation on the income statement shrinks the value of the ovens on the balance sheet by $100,000.</p><p>That is why analysts read the statements together. If profits are supposedly booming but equity and cash are not growing, and the company is not paying the money out, something does not add up. The third statement, cash flow, is where you check. That is next.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A company with $10 billion of total liabilities has $10 billion of debt.', answer: false, explain: 'Liabilities include debt, but also things like unpaid supplier bills, wages owed and deferred revenue. Debt is only the interest-bearing borrowing.' },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each bakery item to its spot on the balance sheet.', pairs: [ { left: 'Money cafés owe for delivered bread', right: 'Current asset' }, { left: 'The commercial ovens', right: 'Long-term asset' }, { left: 'An unpaid flour invoice', right: 'Current liability' }, { left: 'A bank loan due in five years', right: 'Long-term liability' }, { left: 'Profits kept over the years', right: 'Equity' } ] },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Assets', back: 'Everything the company owns or controls that has value.' }, { front: 'Liabilities', back: 'Everything the company owes to others.' }, { front: 'Equity', back: "Assets minus liabilities. The owners' stake on paper." }, { front: 'Retained earnings', back: 'Past profits kept in the business instead of paid out.' }, { front: 'Current ratio', back: 'Current assets divided by current liabilities. A quick liquidity check.' }, { front: 'Net debt', back: 'Total debt minus cash.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Which equation always holds on a balance sheet?', options: ['Assets = Revenue - Expenses', 'Equity = Assets + Liabilities', 'Assets = Liabilities + Equity', 'Liabilities = Assets + Equity'], answer: 2, explain: 'Everything a company owns was funded by creditors (liabilities) or owners (equity).' },
          { q: 'Lumen Bikes has total assets of $80 million and total liabilities of $50 million, with 3 million shares. What is book value per share?', options: ['$10', '$26.67', '$16.67', '$30'], answer: 0, explain: 'Equity = $80M - $50M = $30M. $30M / 3M shares = $10 per share.' },
          { q: 'Which item is a current asset?', options: ['A 10-year bank loan', 'Accounts receivable', 'Retained earnings', 'Goodwill'], answer: 1, explain: 'Receivables should turn into cash within the year. The loan is a liability, retained earnings is equity, and goodwill is a long-term asset.' },
          { q: 'Why can book value understate the worth of a company like a famous brand?', options: ['Brands are always listed twice', 'Book value includes future profits', 'The SEC caps book value', 'Internally built brands and know-how usually do not appear as assets'], answer: 3, explain: 'Accounting rules generally do not record self-created brands and know-how as assets, so book value can miss a lot of real value.' },
          { q: 'A company pays off $1 million of debt using $1 million of cash. What happens to equity?', options: ['It stays the same', 'It rises by $1 million', 'It falls by $1 million', 'It doubles'], answer: 0, explain: 'Assets (cash) and liabilities (debt) both fall by $1 million. Owners are no richer or poorer, so equity is unchanged.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
    {
      id: 'stocks-2-cash-flow',
      title: 'Cash Flow: Where the Money Really Went',
      summary: 'Profit is an opinion, cash is a fact. Learn the three sections of the cash flow statement, free cash flow, and why profitable companies can still run dry.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'coin',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Profitable, but where is the cash?', html: `<p>Ortiz Bakeries earned <strong>$280,000</strong> of net income last year. Yet its bank balance actually <em>fell</em> by $10,000. Ms. Ortiz is confused. Where did the profit go?</p><p>The answer is that profit and cash are measured differently:</p><ul><li>Cafés that bought bread on credit have not all paid yet. Sales counted, cash not received.</li><li>The bakery bought $250,000 of new ovens for the third shop. That cash left this year, but it shows up on the income statement only slowly, through depreciation.</li><li>Depreciation itself reduced profit by $100,000, but no check was written.</li><li>The bakery repaid part of its loan and paid dividends. Neither is an expense, but both use cash.</li></ul><p>The <span class="term" data-def="A report showing the actual cash that came in and went out over a period, split into operating, investing and financing activities.">cash flow statement</span> untangles all of this. It tracks real dollars in and out of the bank.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'wow', say: "Profit is what the accountants say you earned. Cash is what is actually in the bank. They are cousins, not twins." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'cash-flow', caption: 'Cash moves through three channels: running the business, investing in long-term assets, and financing with lenders and owners. Together they explain the change in the bank balance.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Section 1: operating cash flow', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Cash generated by the company's normal business activities: selling products and paying for running costs.">Operating cash flow</span> (CFO) is cash from actually running the bakery. It usually starts from net income and adjusts for the non-cash stuff:</p><table><tbody><tr><td>Net income</td><td>280,000</td></tr><tr><td>Add back depreciation (no cash spent)</td><td>+100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cafés owe more than last year (receivables up)</td><td>-40,000</td></tr><tr><td>More flour on the shelves (inventory up)</td><td>-10,000</td></tr><tr><td>Bakery owes suppliers more (payables up)</td><td>+20,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Operating cash flow</strong></td><td><strong>350,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The logic: if customers owe you more, you booked sales without getting cash, so subtract. If you owe suppliers more, you used their goods without paying yet, so add. Large companies also add back <strong>stock-based compensation</strong>, since it is paid in shares rather than cash.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Why is depreciation added back when calculating operating cash flow?', options: ['Because it is a tax refund', 'Because it reduced net income without any cash leaving this year', 'Because the ovens increased in value', 'Because the SEC requires companies to inflate cash flow'], answer: 1, explain: 'The cash for the ovens was spent when they were bought. Depreciation spreads that old cost over time, so it lowers profit without a new cash payment.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Sections 2 and 3: investing and financing', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Cash spent on or received from long-term assets and investments, such as equipment, acquisitions or securities.">Investing cash flow</span> covers long-term assets. The big one is <span class="term" data-def="Capital expenditures: cash spent on long-lasting physical assets like equipment, buildings or computers.">capital expenditures</span> (capex): the bakery spent <span class="down">-$250,000</span> on ovens and fit-outs for the new shop. Buying another company or selling an old delivery van would land here too.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="Cash flows with lenders and owners: borrowing, repaying debt, issuing shares, buybacks and dividends.">Financing cash flow</span> covers dealings with lenders and owners: the bakery repaid $50,000 of its loan and paid $60,000 of dividends, for <span class="down">-$110,000</span>. New borrowing, issuing shares, and buybacks also live here.</p><table><tbody><tr><td>Operating</td><td>+350,000</td></tr><tr><td>Investing</td><td>-250,000</td></tr><tr><td>Financing</td><td>-110,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Change in cash</strong></td><td><strong>-10,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Mystery solved: the bakery made plenty of cash, then spent it growing, paying down debt and paying owners.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "The three sections always add up to the change in cash on the balance sheet. Another wire connecting the statements." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Lumen Bikes reports operating cash flow of +$12 million, investing cash flow of -$8 million, and financing cash flow of -$1 million. By how many millions of dollars did its cash balance change?', answer: 3, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: '12 - 8 - 1 = +$3 million. The business generated enough cash to fund its investments and its financing payments, with some left over.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Free cash flow: the owner number', html: `<p>Investors love one number above many others:</p><p><span class="hl">Free cash flow = operating cash flow - capital expenditures</span></p><p><span class="term" data-def="Operating cash flow minus capital expenditures. The cash a business produces after investing to maintain and grow itself.">Free cash flow</span> (FCF) is the cash left after the business pays to keep itself running and growing. It is what can go to owners through dividends and buybacks, pay down debt, or pile up for later.</p><p>For the bakery: $350,000 - $250,000 = <strong>$100,000</strong> of FCF, much lower than its $280,000 of net income. Is that bad? Not here. Most of that capex built a new shop, which is <strong>growth capex</strong>. If the bakery stopped expanding, it would only need <strong>maintenance capex</strong> to replace worn ovens, and FCF would jump.</p><p>The question to ask is whether today's spending will produce more cash tomorrow.</p><p>One caution: free cash flow is not an official GAAP line, so data sites and companies calculate it in slightly different ways. Some subtract acquisitions, some do not. When comparing companies, compute it yourself from the cash flow statement so you know exactly what is inside the number.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Pebble Coffee has operating cash flow of $900 million and capital expenditures of $300 million. What is its free cash flow, in millions of dollars?', answer: 600, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: 'FCF = $900M - $300M = $600M. That is cash available for dividends, buybacks, debt payments or saving.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Profitable companies can still go broke', html: `<p>A company can show profits on paper and still run out of cash. It happens when:</p><ul><li><strong>Growth eats cash.</strong> A fast-growing company may need to stock inventory and wait for customers to pay long before cash arrives.</li><li><strong>Customers pay slowly.</strong> If receivables balloon faster than sales, profits are piling up as IOUs.</li><li><strong>Debt comes due</strong> and lenders will not roll it over.</li></ul><p>That is why investors compare <strong>net income with operating cash flow over several years</strong>. For a healthy business, they should roughly track each other. If net income keeps rising while operating cash flow stalls, the profits may be low quality, or worse.</p><p>Also look at how the company funds itself. A business that covers its operating losses year after year by issuing shares or borrowing is relying on outside money to survive.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "When profits rise but cash does not, I start sniffing. Sometimes it is growth. Sometimes it is accounting that will not survive daylight." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: "Dev's favorite cash burner", html: `<p>Dev loves a young electric-scooter company. It has <strong>$300 million</strong> of cash and burns about <strong>$150 million a year</strong> (negative free cash flow). Simple math: at that pace, its <span class="term" data-def="How long a company's cash will last at its current rate of losses. Cash divided by annual cash burn.">cash runway</span> is about two years. Before then, it must start producing cash, borrow, or sell new shares. Dev realizes that if the company sells shares when the price is down, his slice gets diluted hard. Runway is the first number to check for any company that is not yet profitable.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'myth', title: 'Myth: EBITDA is basically cash flow', html: `<p>EBITDA ignores capex, changes in working capital, interest and taxes, all of which take real cash. A business with huge EBITDA can still burn cash if it must keep buying expensive equipment. Free cash flow is the more honest measure of what owners can actually take home.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A company that reports a profit every year cannot run out of cash.', answer: false, explain: 'Profit includes sales not yet collected and excludes spending like capex and debt repayment. A profitable company with slow-paying customers or big debts due can still run dry.' },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each event to the cash flow section where it appears.', pairs: [ { left: 'Cash collected from bread sales', right: 'Operating' }, { left: 'Buying a new delivery van', right: 'Investing' }, { left: 'Paying a dividend', right: 'Financing' }, { left: 'Taking out a new bank loan', right: 'Financing' }, { left: 'Buying another bakery', right: 'Investing' } ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Watch the stock-based comp add-back', html: `<p>Many tech companies report big free cash flow partly because stock-based compensation is added back in operating cash flow. No cash left, true, but owners were diluted. Some investors subtract stock-based compensation from FCF to get a stricter number. Try it both ways when comparing companies that pay heavily in stock.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Operating cash flow', back: 'Cash generated by running the business day to day.' }, { front: 'Capex', back: 'Cash spent on long-lasting assets like equipment and buildings.' }, { front: 'Free cash flow', back: 'Operating cash flow minus capex. Cash available to owners and lenders.' }, { front: 'Financing cash flow', back: 'Borrowing, repaying debt, issuing or buying back shares, paying dividends.' }, { front: 'Growth vs maintenance capex', back: 'Spending to expand vs spending just to keep current operations running.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Which section of the cash flow statement includes paying dividends?', options: ['Operating', 'Investing', 'Financing', 'None, dividends are an expense'], answer: 2, explain: 'Dividends are a payment to owners, which is a financing activity. They are not an expense on the income statement.' },
          { q: 'How is free cash flow most commonly calculated?', options: ['Operating cash flow minus capital expenditures', 'Net income plus dividends', 'Revenue minus cost of goods sold', 'Cash minus debt'], answer: 0, explain: 'FCF = CFO - capex: the cash left after maintaining and growing the business.' },
          { q: 'A company reports rising net income for three years, while operating cash flow falls and receivables soar. What should you suspect?', options: ['The company is extremely conservative', 'Nothing, this is always normal', 'The company has paid off all debt', 'Profits may be piling up as uncollected IOUs, so earnings quality is questionable'], answer: 3, explain: 'When sales are booked but cash never arrives, profit quality is in doubt. It can be innocent, but it deserves digging.' },
          { q: 'Why might a fast-growing company show free cash flow far below its net income?', options: ['It is spending heavily on capex and working capital to expand', 'Depreciation is too low', 'It pays no taxes', 'Free cash flow ignores capex'], answer: 0, explain: 'Growth often needs new equipment, inventory and credit to customers, all of which use cash before profits arrive in cash form.' },
          { q: 'Ortiz Bakeries buys a $40,000 oven with cash. In the year of purchase, what happens?', options: ['Net income falls by $40,000 immediately', 'Investing cash flow shows -$40,000, and the cost hits profit gradually through depreciation', 'Financing cash flow shows +$40,000', 'Nothing appears anywhere until the oven breaks'], answer: 1, explain: "The cash leaves at once and shows as capex in investing. On the income statement, the cost is spread over the oven's useful life as depreciation." },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
    {
      id: 'stocks-2-earnings-season',
      title: 'Earnings Season: Beats, Misses and Big Moves',
      summary: 'Four times a year companies report their results. Learn EPS, consensus estimates, guidance, SEC filings, and why a stock can fall on good news.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'target',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Report card day', html: `<p>Public companies in the US report results every quarter. Because most companies end their quarters in March, June, September and December, reports bunch up in the weeks that follow. That rush is called <span class="term" data-def="The weeks after each quarter ends, when most public companies report their results.">earnings season</span>, and it happens four times a year.</p><p>Lumen Bikes is now public and reports today, right after the market closes at 4:00 p.m. Eastern. Many companies report either before the open or after the close, so the news lands when the regular session is shut. The stock then reacts in extended-hours trading, where fewer people trade and prices can jump around.</p><p>Dev has his app open, refreshing every three seconds. Maya, who owns a few shares, is at work. One of them will have a better evening. By the end of this lesson, you will know what both of them should actually look at.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Earnings day is report card day. Except the parents are millions of investors and they have already guessed your grades." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'EPS: basic vs diluted', html: `<p>The headline number is <strong>earnings per share</strong>. There are two versions:</p><ul><li><span class="term" data-def="Net income divided by the weighted average number of shares actually outstanding.">Basic EPS</span> = net income / average shares outstanding.</li><li><span class="term" data-def="EPS calculated as if stock options, restricted stock units and convertible securities had all become shares. Usually slightly lower than basic EPS.">Diluted EPS</span> pretends that stock options, restricted stock units and convertible bonds that could become shares already have.</li></ul><p>Lumen earned <strong>$10 million</strong> this quarter with <strong>20 million</strong> shares outstanding, so basic EPS is $0.50. But employees hold options and stock units that could add <strong>1 million</strong> more shares. Diluted EPS = $10M / 21M = about <strong>$0.48</strong>.</p><p>Diluted EPS is the more conservative number and the one most analysts quote. If basic and diluted are far apart, a lot of future dilution is waiting in the wings.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Pebble Coffee earned $90 million this quarter. It has 300 million shares outstanding plus 10 million shares worth of options and stock units. What is diluted EPS, in dollars?', answer: 0.29, tolerance: 0.005, unit: '$', explain: 'Diluted shares = 300M + 10M = 310M. $90M / 310M is about $0.29. Basic EPS would be $0.30.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Beat or miss?', html: `<p>Before the report, Wall Street analysts publish their own estimates. The average is the <span class="term" data-def="The average of analysts' forecasts for a company's results, such as EPS or revenue.">consensus estimate</span>. The market judges results against that consensus, not against zero.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Consensus</th><th>Actual</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Diluted EPS</td><td>$0.45</td><td>$0.48</td><td><span class="up">Beat</span></td></tr><tr><td>Revenue</td><td>$60M</td><td>$58M</td><td><span class="down">Miss</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>So did Lumen do well? Mixed. EPS <strong>beat</strong>, but revenue <strong>missed</strong>. Investors dig into why. Maybe Lumen cut marketing costs to protect profit while sales slowed. That kind of beat is lower quality than one driven by booming demand.</p><p>Beats are actually very common, partly because companies steer analysts toward expectations they can clear. So a tiny beat often means little. What matters is the size, the source, and what comes next.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Consensus expected EPS of $1.20. The company reports $1.10, which is 25% higher than last year. How will most headlines describe this?', options: ['A beat, because profit grew 25%', 'In line, because growth was positive', 'A miss, because it came in below consensus', 'Impossible to say without the share price'], answer: 2, explain: 'Beats and misses are measured against expectations, not last year. Growing 25% can still be a miss if analysts expected more.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Guidance and the call', html: `<p>After the numbers come two more pieces, and they often move the stock more than the results themselves.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="A company's own forecast for future results, such as next quarter's revenue or full-year EPS.">Guidance</span> is management's forecast for the next quarter or year. A stock price is a bet on the future, so if Lumen beats this quarter but lowers its full-year sales forecast, the future just got worse.</p><p>Then there is the <strong>earnings call</strong>, usually an hour-long conference call or webcast where executives discuss results and answer analysts' questions. Anyone can listen, and transcripts are widely available. Listen for:</p><ul><li>What changed in demand, pricing and costs</li><li>Answers that dodge a direct question</li><li>New risks, like a lost customer or supply problem</li></ul><p>Tone matters too. Investors notice when a usually confident CEO suddenly sounds very careful.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why stocks fall on good news', html: `<p>Lumen beat on EPS. The stock drops <span class="down">-9%</span> after hours. Dev is outraged. What happened?</p><ul><li><strong>Expectations were higher than consensus.</strong> The stock had already risen 30% into the report. Investors were privately hoping for a blowout, and good was not good enough.</li><li><strong>Weak guidance.</strong> The forecast for next quarter came in below what analysts expected.</li><li><strong>Low-quality beat.</strong> Profit came from cost cuts or a one-time tax benefit, not from growing sales.</li><li><strong>A key metric slipped.</strong> Margins narrowed, or a closely watched number like new customers fell short.</li></ul><p>The stock price already contains the market's guess about the future. It moves on the <em>surprise</em>, the gap between what happened and what was expected. That is why "buy the rumor, sell the news" is a saying.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Holding a stock through earnings is a coin flip on a surprise. Moves of 10% or more overnight happen. Size positions so a gap will not wreck you." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'Maya vs Dev on earnings night', html: `<p>Dev bought Lumen options an hour before the report, betting on a pop. The 9% drop wiped out most of his bet overnight. Maya owns her shares as a long-term owner. She reads the release the next morning, checks whether sales growth and margins are still on track, and asks whether the long-term story changed. One quarter rarely changes what a business is worth. Dev learned that a surprise is, by definition, impossible to predict reliably.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If a company reports record profits, its stock price will rise.', answer: false, explain: 'Prices react to results versus expectations and to guidance. Record profits that fall short of what was expected, or come with weak guidance, can send a stock lower.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The paper trail: SEC filings', html: `<p>Press releases are marketing. The legal documents live on the SEC's free database, <span class="term" data-def="The SEC's free online system where public companies file their official reports.">EDGAR</span>. The big three:</p><ul><li><strong>10-K:</strong> the annual report. Audited financial statements, a business description, risk factors, and management's discussion of results. Usually long and very useful.</li><li><strong>10-Q:</strong> the quarterly report, filed for the first three quarters (the 10-K covers the fourth). Financials are reviewed by auditors but not fully audited.</li><li><strong>8-K:</strong> filed for major events, like a CEO leaving, a big acquisition, or a bankruptcy, generally within four business days. Companies also typically furnish their earnings press release on an 8-K.</li></ul><p>Rules in flux: quarterly reporting has been the US standard for decades, but in 2025 regulators began considering letting companies report every six months instead. Check the current SEC rule before assuming quarterly reports.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'think', say: "Rule check: 10-K yearly, 10-Q quarterly, 8-K for big events. Deadlines depend on company size. Verify current rules on the SEC site." } },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each SEC filing to what it is.', pairs: [ { left: '10-K', right: 'Annual report with audited financials' }, { left: '10-Q', right: 'Quarterly report for the first three quarters' }, { left: '8-K', right: 'Report of a major event, filed within days' }, { left: 'S-1', right: 'Registration statement before an IPO' } ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'How to read a 10-K without crying', html: `<p>You do not have to read all of it. Start with:</p><ol><li><strong>Business:</strong> how the company actually makes money.</li><li><strong>Risk Factors:</strong> what management and lawyers worry about. Skim for anything specific rather than generic.</li><li><strong>MD&A:</strong> management's discussion and analysis of the year.</li><li><strong>The three statements and their notes:</strong> the notes are where details like debt maturities and lawsuits hide.</li></ol><p>Compare this year's risk factors with last year's. New ones are often the most informative.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Consensus estimate', back: "The average of analysts' forecasts for a result like EPS." }, { front: 'Beat / miss', back: 'Results above or below the consensus estimate.' }, { front: 'Guidance', back: "Management's forecast of future results." }, { front: 'Diluted EPS', back: 'EPS counting shares that options, stock units and convertibles could create.' }, { front: 'EDGAR', back: 'The SEC free database of company filings.' }, { front: '8-K', back: 'A filing announcing a major event, generally within four business days.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Why is diluted EPS usually a bit lower than basic EPS?', options: ['It subtracts dividends', 'It includes shares that options, stock units and convertibles could create', "It uses last year's profits", 'It excludes one-time gains'], answer: 1, explain: 'Diluted EPS divides the same profit by a larger share count that includes potential shares.' },
          { q: 'Lumen beats EPS estimates but cuts its full-year revenue guidance. The stock falls 8%. What is the most likely reason?', options: ['The SEC penalized the beat', 'Investors care more about the weaker future outlook than the past quarter', 'EPS beats always cause drops', 'The market was closed'], answer: 1, explain: 'Stock prices are bets on the future. Lower guidance reduces expected future profits, which can outweigh a past beat.' },
          { q: "Where can you read a company's official annual report for free?", options: ['Only through a paid terminal', 'Only at the annual meeting', 'On the SEC EDGAR database, as the 10-K', 'On the 8-K filed each January'], answer: 2, explain: 'EDGAR hosts 10-Ks, 10-Qs, 8-Ks and more, free for anyone.' },
          { q: 'What does "beat" mean in earnings season?', options: ['Results came in above the consensus estimate', 'The stock rose after the report', 'Profit grew compared with last year', 'The company raised its dividend'], answer: 0, explain: "A beat compares results with analysts' expectations. It does not guarantee the stock rises." },
          { q: 'A company announces its CEO resigned effective immediately. Which filing would typically disclose this within days?', options: ['10-K', 'S-1', '10-Q', '8-K'], answer: 3, explain: 'The 8-K is for material events between regular reports, and is generally due within four business days.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
    {
      id: 'stocks-2-valuation-ratios',
      title: 'Is It Cheap? Valuation Ratios',
      summary: 'P/E, forward P/E, PEG, price-to-sales, price-to-book, EV/EBITDA and earnings yield: what each one measures, when it works, and when it lies.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'calculator',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Price means nothing alone', html: `<p>Back in chapter 1, Dev learned that a $25 stock is not automatically cheaper than a $500 one. To judge price, you compare it with what you get for it: earnings, sales, cash flow or assets. Those comparisons are <span class="term" data-def="Ratios comparing a stock's price (or the company's total value) with a fundamental like earnings, sales or book value.">valuation ratios</span>, sometimes called multiples.</p><p>Think of them as a price-per-pound sticker at the grocery store. A 5-pound bag for $10 and a 1-pound bag for $3 are hard to compare until you see $2 per pound vs $3 per pound. Ratios do the same for stocks.</p><p>No single ratio tells the whole story. Each one shines in some situations and misleads in others. This lesson gives you the main toolkit and, just as important, the warning label for each tool.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Ratios are price-per-pound stickers for companies. Same idea, more decimals." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'P/E: trailing vs forward', html: `<p>The most famous ratio is <span class="term" data-def="Price-to-earnings ratio: share price divided by earnings per share.">price-to-earnings</span>:</p><p><span class="hl">P/E = share price / earnings per share</span></p><p>Say Ortiz Bakeries shares trade at <strong>$42</strong>. Last year's EPS was <strong>$2.80</strong>. P/E = 42 / 2.80 = <strong>15</strong>. Investors are paying $15 for each $1 of yearly profit.</p><p>Which earnings, though?</p><ul><li><span class="term" data-def="P/E using the last 12 months of actual reported earnings. Often labeled TTM.">Trailing P/E</span> uses the last 12 months of actual results. Real numbers, but backward-looking.</li><li><span class="term" data-def="P/E using analysts' estimates of the next 12 months or next fiscal year's earnings.">Forward P/E</span> uses estimated earnings for the next year. More relevant to the future, but only as good as the estimate.</li></ul><p>If analysts expect the bakery's EPS to rise to $3.20, the forward P/E is 42 / 3.20, about <strong>13</strong>. A forward P/E well below the trailing one means profits are expected to grow.</p>` },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'pe-ratio', props: { price: 42, eps: 2.8 }, caption: 'Move the price and EPS and watch the P/E change. Notice how a small drop in earnings can make the same price look much more expensive.' },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Lumen Bikes trades at $54 with trailing 12-month EPS of $3.00. What is its trailing P/E?', answer: 18, tolerance: 0.05, unit: '', explain: "$54 / $3.00 = 18. Investors pay $18 for each $1 of last year's profit." },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Flip it: earnings yield', html: `<p>Turn P/E upside down and you get the <span class="term" data-def="Earnings per share divided by price. The inverse of P/E, shown as a percentage.">earnings yield</span>:</p><p><span class="hl">Earnings yield = EPS / price = 1 / (P/E)</span></p><p>The bakery at a P/E of 15 has an earnings yield of 1 / 15, about <strong>6.7%</strong>. That means each $100 invested "owns" about $6.70 of yearly profit, whether or not it is paid out.</p><p>Earnings yield is handy for comparing stocks with bonds. If safe government bonds yield 4%, a stock with a 6.7% earnings yield offers more profit per dollar, but with far less certainty, and no promise you ever receive it. A stock with a P/E of 50 has an earnings yield of just 2%, so its owners are betting heavily on growth.</p><p>One limit: if a company loses money, P/E becomes meaningless, since there is no positive E to divide by. You need other ratios for those.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'PEG: adding growth', html: `<p>A high P/E is not automatically expensive if earnings are growing fast. The <span class="term" data-def="P/E ratio divided by the expected annual earnings growth rate in percent.">PEG ratio</span> tries to account for that:</p><p><span class="hl">PEG = P/E / expected annual EPS growth (in %)</span></p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>P/E</th><th>Growth</th><th>PEG</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lumen Bikes</td><td>30</td><td>20%</td><td>1.5</td></tr><tr><td>Pebble Coffee</td><td>15</td><td>5%</td><td>3.0</td></tr></tbody></table><p>By P/E, Pebble looks cheaper. By PEG, Lumen does. Fund manager Peter Lynch popularized the idea that a P/E around a company's growth rate (a PEG near 1) can be a reasonable price.</p><p>The warning label: PEG depends on a growth forecast, and forecasts are often wrong, especially optimistic ones for hot companies. It also ignores dividends and risk.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "PEG is only as good as the growth number you plug in. Garbage growth estimate, garbage PEG." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'A company has a P/E of 24 and analysts expect earnings to grow 16% a year. What is its PEG ratio?', answer: 1.5, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '', explain: 'PEG = 24 / 16 = 1.5.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'P/S and P/B', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Market cap divided by annual revenue, or price per share divided by sales per share.">Price-to-sales</span> (P/S) = market cap / revenue. It works when earnings are negative or temporarily depressed, like a young company or a cyclical one in a bad year. The catch: sales are not profit. A company with thin margins deserves a far lower P/S than one with fat margins.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="Share price divided by book value per share.">Price-to-book</span> (P/B) = price / book value per share. The bakery at $42 with $13 of book value per share has a P/B of about <strong>3.2</strong>. P/B is most useful for businesses whose assets are mostly financial and marked close to their real value, like banks and insurers. For software or brand-driven companies, book value misses most of the real assets, so P/B says little.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'EV/EBITDA: debt-aware', html: `<p>P/E looks only at the equity. Two companies with the same P/E can carry very different amounts of debt. <span class="term" data-def="Enterprise value divided by EBITDA. Compares the whole business value, including debt, with operating earnings before depreciation.">EV/EBITDA</span> fixes that by comparing the price of the <em>whole</em> business with its operating earnings:</p><p><span class="hl">EV/EBITDA = (market cap + debt - cash) / EBITDA</span></p><p>Pebble Coffee has an enterprise value of $9 billion and EBITDA of $900 million, so EV/EBITDA = <strong>10</strong>. Because EV includes debt and EBITDA comes before interest, this ratio lets you compare companies with different borrowing levels more fairly. It is popular in buyouts and acquisitions.</p><p>Remember the warning from the cash flow lesson, though. EBITDA ignores capex. For equipment-hungry businesses, like airlines or telecom networks, a low EV/EBITDA can hide heavy ongoing spending.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Which valuation ratio is usually most informative for a bank?', options: ['Price-to-sales', 'Price-to-book', 'PEG', 'EV/EBITDA'], answer: 1, explain: 'A bank balance sheet is mostly loans and securities, recorded close to their value, so book value is meaningful. EBITDA makes little sense for a lender, since interest is its core business.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Cheap compared to what?', html: `<p>A ratio by itself is just a number. Its meaning comes from comparison:</p><ul><li><strong>Against peers in the same industry.</strong> Software companies often trade at much higher P/Es than utilities, because they grow faster and need less capital. Compare a bakery with other food companies, not with a chipmaker.</li><li><strong>Against its own history.</strong> If a company has traded between 15 and 25 times earnings for a decade and now sits at 12, ask what changed.</li><li><strong>Against its growth and quality.</strong> A higher multiple can be fair for a business that grows faster, earns more on its capital, and carries little debt.</li></ul><p>And always ask the scariest question: is it cheap because the market is wrong, or because the market knows something? A low P/E often means investors expect profits to fall. Sometimes they are right.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "A P/E of 6 is not a bargain if earnings are about to halve. Then it was really a P/E of 12 in disguise." } },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each ratio to how it is calculated.', pairs: [ { left: 'P/E', right: 'Price / earnings per share' }, { left: 'Earnings yield', right: 'Earnings per share / price' }, { left: 'PEG', right: 'P/E / expected growth rate' }, { left: 'P/B', right: 'Price / book value per share' }, { left: 'EV/EBITDA', right: 'Enterprise value / EBITDA' } ] },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Trailing P/E', back: 'Price divided by the last 12 months of actual EPS.' }, { front: 'Forward P/E', back: 'Price divided by estimated EPS for the next year.' }, { front: 'Earnings yield', back: 'EPS divided by price. The inverse of P/E.' }, { front: 'PEG', back: 'P/E divided by expected growth rate. Tries to adjust P/E for growth.' }, { front: 'P/S', back: 'Market cap divided by revenue. Useful when earnings are negative.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'A stock trades at $80 with expected next-year EPS of $5 and trailing EPS of $4. What is its forward P/E?', options: ['20', '16', '25', '4'], answer: 1, explain: 'Forward P/E uses expected earnings: $80 / $5 = 16. The trailing P/E would be $80 / $4 = 20.' },
          { q: 'A stock has a P/E of 25. What is its earnings yield?', options: ['25%', '2.5%', '0.25%', '4%'], answer: 3, explain: 'Earnings yield = 1 / 25 = 0.04, or 4%.' },
          { q: 'Why might an analyst use price-to-sales instead of P/E?', options: ['The company currently has negative earnings', 'The company has no revenue', 'P/S includes debt', 'P/S is required by the SEC'], answer: 0, explain: 'When earnings are negative or temporarily depressed, P/E is meaningless or misleading, so sales can be a more stable base. Margins still matter.' },
          { q: 'Company A and Company B have identical P/E ratios, but A has huge debt and B has none. Which ratio better captures the difference?', options: ['Dividend yield', 'Price-to-sales', 'EV/EBITDA', 'PEG'], answer: 2, explain: 'Enterprise value includes debt, so EV/EBITDA reflects that buying all of A also means taking on its borrowings.' },
          { q: 'A utility trades at a P/E of 16 and a software company at 35. What is the best conclusion?', options: ['The utility is definitely the better buy', 'The software company is definitely overvalued', 'They should have the same P/E', 'Comparing across industries is misleading; compare each with its peers, growth and history'], answer: 3, explain: 'Different industries have different growth, capital needs and risks, so their normal multiples differ. Compare like with like.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
    {
      id: 'stocks-2-quality-metrics',
      title: 'Is It Any Good? Quality Metrics and Red Flags',
      summary: 'Margins, ROE, ROIC, debt-to-equity, current ratio and interest coverage tell you how strong a business is. Plus the red flags that should make you dig deeper.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'shield',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Cheap is not the same as good', html: `<p>Valuation ratios tell you what you are <em>paying</em>. Quality metrics tell you what you are <em>getting</em>. A cheap stock attached to a fragile, low-profit business can be a terrible deal, while a fairly priced stock of an excellent business can be a great one.</p><p>Quality metrics answer three questions:</p><ol><li><strong>How profitable is each dollar of sales?</strong> Margins.</li><li><strong>How well does the company use the money invested in it?</strong> Returns on equity and capital.</li><li><strong>Could it survive a bad year?</strong> Debt and liquidity ratios.</li></ol><p>We will check all three on Ortiz Bakeries, using the statements from earlier in this chapter: $2.0 million of revenue, $400,000 of operating income, $280,000 of net income, $1.3 million of equity and $500,000 of debt.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: "Price tells you what you pay. Quality tells you what you get. You need both before you can call anything a bargain." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Three margins', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>Margin</th><th>Formula</th><th>Bakery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gross</td><td>Gross profit / revenue</td><td>60%</td></tr><tr><td>Operating</td><td>Operating income / revenue</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>Net</td><td>Net income / revenue</td><td>14%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each margin tells a story. <strong>Gross margin</strong> shows pricing power and production costs: can the bakery charge well above what ingredients cost? <strong>Operating margin</strong> shows how efficiently the whole business runs. <strong>Net margin</strong> shows what is left after lenders and taxes.</p><p>The trend matters more than any one year. Rising margins can signal pricing power or growing scale. Shrinking margins can signal rising competition, costs that cannot be passed on, or a price war. And remember, compare margins with peers in the same industry. A grocer and a software company live in different universes.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Lumen Bikes has revenue of $50 million and operating income of $6 million. What is its operating margin, in percent?', answer: 12, tolerance: 0.1, unit: '%', explain: '$6M / $50M = 0.12, or 12%. Out of every dollar of bike sales, 12 cents is left after running the business, before interest and taxes.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Return on equity', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Net income divided by shareholders' equity. How much profit the company earns on the owners' accounting stake.">Return on equity</span> (ROE) asks: for every dollar of owners' capital on the books, how much profit does the business earn?</p><p><span class="hl">ROE = net income / shareholders' equity</span></p><p>Bakery: $280,000 / $1,300,000 = about <strong>21.5%</strong>. Each dollar of equity earns about 21 cents a year. Strong. (Analysts often use average equity over the year; we will keep it simple.)</p><p>The catch: ROE can be pumped up with <strong>debt</strong>. If a company borrows heavily and uses the cash to buy back shares, equity shrinks, and ROE rises, even though the business did not improve. It just got riskier. Some companies with negative equity show meaningless or even negative ROE figures. So always check ROE alongside debt.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "High ROE with low debt is impressive. High ROE with mountains of debt is a magic trick. Find out which one you are watching." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'Two bakeries, same ovens', html: `<p>Bakery A and Bakery B each need $1 million of capital and each earn $150,000 a year before interest. A is funded entirely by its owners: ROE = $150,000 / $1,000,000 = <strong>15%</strong>. B borrows $800,000 at 5% ($40,000 interest) and owners put in only $200,000. Ignoring taxes, B earns $110,000 on $200,000 of equity, a <strong>55% ROE</strong>. Same ovens, same customers. B just took on far more risk. If sales dip 30%, A shrugs. B may struggle to pay the bank.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: "Pebble Coffee earned $600 million of net income and has $3 billion of shareholders' equity. What is its ROE, in percent?", answer: 20, tolerance: 0.1, unit: '%', explain: '$600M / $3,000M = 0.20, or 20%.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'ROIC: the debt-proof version', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Return on invested capital: after-tax operating profit divided by the total capital (debt plus equity, often net of cash) invested in the business.">ROIC</span>, return on invested capital, fixes ROE's blind spot by counting <em>all</em> the money invested, from lenders and owners alike:</p><p><span class="hl">ROIC = after-tax operating profit / (debt + equity - cash)</span></p><p>Bakery: operating income of $400,000, minus taxes at its roughly 24% rate, gives about $303,000. Invested capital is $500,000 + $1,300,000 - $360,000 = $1,440,000. ROIC is about <strong>21%</strong>.</p><p>Why investors love ROIC: a business that earns a high return on capital, and can keep reinvesting at that rate, compounds value fast. If ROIC is below what that capital costs (what lenders and investors demand, often somewhere around high single digits), growth actually <em>destroys</em> value. Exact formulas vary between data providers, so compare like with like.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Can it survive a bad year?', html: `<p>Three numbers check the shock absorbers:</p><ul><li><span class="term" data-def="Total debt divided by shareholders' equity. How much the company leans on borrowing.">Debt-to-equity</span> = total debt / equity. Bakery: $500,000 / $1,300,000 = <strong>0.38</strong>. Modest borrowing.</li><li><strong>Current ratio</strong> = current assets / current liabilities. Bakery: <strong>2.0</strong>. Comfortable for bills due within a year.</li><li><span class="term" data-def="Operating income (EBIT) divided by interest expense. How many times over the company can pay its interest.">Interest coverage</span> = operating income / interest expense. Bakery: $400,000 / $30,000 = about <strong>13 times</strong>. Profits could fall a long way before interest became a problem.</li></ul><p>There are no magic cutoffs, and normal levels differ by industry. Utilities routinely carry lots of debt against steady income. But as a rough feel, interest coverage under about 2 or 3, or a current ratio well under 1 with little cash, deserves a closer look.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'A company has operating income of $20 million and interest expense of $8 million. What is its interest coverage ratio?', answer: 2.5, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '', explain: '$20M / $8M = 2.5. A 60% drop in operating income would leave it unable to cover interest from profits. That is a thin cushion.' },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Which company looks most financially fragile?', options: ['Debt-to-equity 0.2, interest coverage 15x, current ratio 2.1', 'Debt-to-equity 0.5, interest coverage 9x, current ratio 1.6', 'Debt-to-equity 3.0, interest coverage 1.4x, current ratio 0.7', 'No debt, current ratio 3.0'], answer: 2, explain: 'Heavy debt, barely enough profit to pay interest, and fewer current assets than current liabilities is a dangerous mix if business slows.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Red flags worth digging into', html: `<p>None of these prove fraud or failure. Each one is a reason to read more carefully:</p><ul><li><strong>Profits up, cash flow down</strong>, year after year.</li><li><strong>Receivables or inventory growing much faster than sales.</strong> Customers not paying, or products not selling.</li><li><strong>Adjusted profits always far above GAAP profits</strong>, with "one-time" charges every year.</li><li><strong>Share count climbing</strong> steadily from heavy stock-based pay or repeated offerings.</li><li><strong>Debt rising faster than earnings</strong>, especially if used to pay dividends or buybacks.</li><li><strong>A change of auditor, a late filing, or a restatement</strong> of past results.</li><li><strong>Big goodwill write-downs</strong>, meaning past acquisitions disappointed.</li><li><strong>Executives selling large amounts of stock</strong> while talking up the future.</li></ul>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "One red flag is a question. Three red flags is an answer. Accounting scandals usually showed several of these before the headlines." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Do it yourself, then check', html: `<p>Finance sites show these ratios ready-made, but they define them differently. One site's ROIC or debt-to-equity can differ a lot from another's, for example on whether leases count as debt. Calculate the key ones yourself from the 10-K at least once for any company you study closely. You will understand the business better, and you will spot when a number looks off.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A company with a very high ROE is always a high-quality business.', answer: false, explain: 'ROE can be inflated by heavy debt or by buybacks that shrink equity. Check debt-to-equity and ROIC to see whether the returns come from the business or from leverage.' },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each metric to the question it answers.', pairs: [ { left: 'Gross margin', right: 'How much is left after making the product?' }, { left: 'ROIC', right: 'How well is all invested capital being used?' }, { left: 'Interest coverage', right: 'Can profits comfortably pay the interest?' }, { left: 'Current ratio', right: 'Can it pay bills due within a year?' }, { left: 'Debt-to-equity', right: 'How much does it lean on borrowing?' } ] },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Operating margin', back: 'Operating income divided by revenue.' }, { front: 'ROE', back: "Net income divided by shareholders' equity. Can be inflated by debt." }, { front: 'ROIC', back: 'After-tax operating profit divided by all invested capital.' }, { front: 'Debt-to-equity', back: 'Total debt divided by equity.' }, { front: 'Interest coverage', back: 'Operating income divided by interest expense.' }, { front: 'Restatement', back: 'Correcting previously reported financial results. Often a serious warning sign.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: "Lumen Bikes' gross margin fell from 35% to 28% over three years while revenue grew. What is a likely explanation to investigate?", options: ['The company is paying less tax', 'Rising parts costs or price cuts to win sales', 'More shares were issued', 'Interest rates fell'], answer: 1, explain: 'Gross margin reflects pricing and direct production costs. A slide often means costs rose or prices were cut, perhaps due to competition.' },
          { q: 'Company X boosted ROE from 15% to 30% in two years with flat profits. What most likely happened?', options: ['Sales doubled', 'Gross margin doubled', 'Taxes fell to zero', 'Equity shrank, possibly from debt-funded buybacks'], answer: 3, explain: 'With profits flat, ROE can only double if equity roughly halved. Debt-funded buybacks are a common way that happens.' },
          { q: 'Why do many investors prefer ROIC over ROE?', options: ['ROIC counts both debt and equity capital, so leverage cannot easily inflate it', 'ROIC is always higher', 'ROIC ignores taxes', 'ROIC is required by GAAP'], answer: 0, explain: 'ROIC measures returns on all capital invested, making it harder to flatter with borrowing.' },
          { q: 'Which combination is the biggest red flag?', options: ['Net income and operating cash flow growing together', 'A slowly falling share count', 'Rising net income, falling operating cash flow and ballooning receivables', 'Stable margins and modest debt'], answer: 2, explain: 'Profits that never turn into cash, with customers owing more and more, are a classic warning sign of low-quality earnings.' },
          { q: 'The bakery has operating income of $400,000 and interest expense of $30,000. Its interest coverage is about...', options: ['13x', '1.3x', '7.5x', '30x'], answer: 0, explain: '$400,000 / $30,000 is about 13.3, so profits cover interest more than 13 times.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  bossQuestions: [
    { q: 'A company reports net income of $50M, depreciation of $20M, a $30M increase in receivables, and capex of $25M. Ignoring other items, what is its free cash flow?', options: ['$15M', '$45M', '$70M', '$40M'], answer: 0, explain: 'Operating cash flow = $50M + $20M - $30M = $40M. Free cash flow = $40M - $25M = $15M, far below net income because receivables soaked up cash.' },
    { q: 'Lumen Bikes trades at $60. Trailing EPS is $3, expected EPS next year is $4, and long-term growth is expected at 15% a year. Which set of numbers is correct?', options: ['Trailing P/E 15, forward P/E 20, PEG 1.0', 'Trailing P/E 20, forward P/E 15, PEG based on trailing P/E about 1.33', 'Trailing P/E 20, forward P/E 15, earnings yield 20%', 'Trailing P/E 3, forward P/E 4, PEG 0.2'], answer: 1, explain: 'Trailing P/E = 60 / 3 = 20. Forward P/E = 60 / 4 = 15. PEG = 20 / 15 is about 1.33. Earnings yield on trailing EPS is 5%.' },
    { q: 'A company beats EPS consensus by 10 cents, but the beat came from a one-time tax benefit, revenue missed, and full-year guidance was cut. Which reaction makes the most sense?', options: ['A big rally, because it beat EPS', 'No reaction, because tax items are ignored', 'A likely drop, because underlying sales and the outlook weakened', 'A trading halt by the SEC'], answer: 2, explain: 'A low-quality beat plus a revenue miss and weaker guidance means expected future profits fell. Markets trade on the future and the surprise.' },
    { q: 'Company A has ROE of 40% and debt-to-equity of 4.0. Company B has ROE of 18%, ROIC of 17% and almost no debt. Which statement is most reasonable?', options: ['A is clearly the higher-quality business', 'B is worse because its ROE is lower', 'Debt has no effect on ROE', "A's ROE is likely boosted by leverage, so B may have the stronger underlying economics"], answer: 3, explain: 'Heavy borrowing shrinks equity and inflates ROE. B earns solid returns on all its capital without leverage, which is a sturdier sign of quality.' },
    { q: 'Ortiz Bakeries has $2.0M of assets, $700K of liabilities, 100,000 shares, and trades at $39 a share. What is its price-to-book ratio?', options: ['3.0', '1.95', '5.6', '0.33'], answer: 0, explain: 'Equity = $2.0M - $0.7M = $1.3M, so book value per share = $13. P/B = $39 / $13 = 3.0.' },
  ],
};
